#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055905} :activity/id #uuid "93eee4d2-ab33-5b39-b0ee-143bb773c7c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065 :topic/id #uuid "d7019ce6-160a-5aa4-9e08-78782f485dfa" :topic/math-academy-id 45} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056036 :tutorial/content "A **set** is a collection of objects known as **elements** or **members**.\n\nUsually, the elements of a set have some property in common. For example, a set could be a class at school where the students are the members of the set. The common property is that all the students are members of the class.\n\nTo write a set, we list the elements between two braces (curly brackets). Commas are used to separate the elements.\n\nSo, for example, the set of the primary colors would be written as\n\n$$\nS =\\{\\text{yellow}, \\: \\text{red}, \\: \\text{blue} \\}\n$$\n\nThe elements of this set are $\\text{yellow}$, $\\text{red}$, and $\\text{blue}$.\n\nA set that does not contain any elements is known as the **null set** or **empty set** and is written as either\n$\\emptyset \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\{\\}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- *A set may contain other sets as elements.* For instance, the set\n$T = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3, \\: 4, \\: \\{5, 6\\}\\}$\ncontains the set $\\{5, 6\\}$ as an element. However, it does *not* contain the elements $5$ and $6$.\n- *Order doesn't matter.* The order in which the elements are listed is irrelevant. For instance, the following sets represent the same thing:\n$A = \\{1, \\: 2, \\: 3 \\} = \\{2, \\: 3, \\: 1\\}$\nIt's usually easier to understand a set if the elements are listed in some order.\n- *Duplicates don't matter.* Having multiple copies of an element does not affect the structure of a set. For example, the following sets represent the same thing:\n$B = \\{m, \\: n, \\: p, \\: q\\} = \\{m, \\: m, \\: m, \\: n, \\: n, \\: p, \\: p, \\: p, \\: q\\}$\nWhen writing a set, we should try to organize it so that each element is listed once only." :tutorial/id #uuid "d42ef3b1-d046-5e5d-b671-e5bb3eee9963" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8211 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ea6b6835-3d1a-5b28-9df6-f7c82c95a7a7" :step/math-academy-id 86199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054425 :question/id #uuid "16e0f524-49b5-549d-8e60-8597379c4350" :question/math-academy-id "e-13134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33551349-bb39-5a79-bf56-a4df753201ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055240}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "59430871-5d21-53f3-a1dc-d90c496d02fa" :step/math-academy-id 86200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054426 :question/id #uuid "046f7284-3715-555c-bdc9-c108db061205" :question/math-academy-id "e-13135"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de59db4d-82d5-5bad-a559-bf5396375355" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055499}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Sets"} :step/id #uuid "b8cc6fa4-8144-59f9-8c08-a90356ab153e" :step/math-academy-id 86204 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056037 :tutorial/content "By convention, sets are denoted by uppercase letters, while lowercase letters refer to elements of the set.\n\nThere is a special notation, $\\in$, for expressing elements of sets. For example, if $a$ is an element of a set $A$, we write\n\n$$\na \\in A\n$$\n\nFor example, for the set $S$, given by\n\n$$\nS = \\{\\text{yellow}, \\text{red}, \\text{blue} \\}\n$$\n\nwe can write\n\n$$\n\\text{yellow}\\in S\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $a$ is *not* an element of $A$, then we write\n\n$$\na \\notin A\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\text{green} \\notin S\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2d5957fc-01d1-567b-8834-752b6a1b99a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8212 :tutorial/title "Element Notation"} :step/id #uuid "fbc9e243-fffa-5f93-9ca4-244251573ebd" :step/math-academy-id 86208 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054427 :question/id #uuid "5404d545-570b-53d7-b16a-b23a371d4abd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900f0d3d-6a5c-53b2-b987-eee1a8963b6b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054973}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Element Notation"} :step/id #uuid "1dd5dac0-5f14-54bf-a05b-c4a8f4ce5b61" :step/math-academy-id 86209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055910}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055910 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056038 :tutorial/content "When a set contains only a few elements, we can write them all explicitly.\n\nFor example, the set $A$ containing all positive even integers less than $20$ could be written as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: 10, \\: 12, \\: 14, \\: 16, \\: 18\\}\n$$\n\nHowever, when a set contains many elements, we can save ourselves the pain of writing out all its elements using an ellipsis, \"$\\ldots$\".\n\nFor example:\n\n- The set $B$ containing all positive even integers less than $200$ can be written as\n$B = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots, \\: 198\\}$.\n- The set $C$ containing *all* positive even integers can be written as\n$C = \\{2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: \\ldots \\}$.\n\nGenerally, an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element or \"and so on\" if not.\n\nFinally, note that $A$ and $B$ contain a finite number of elements ($A$ and $B$ contain $9$ and $99$ elements, respectively), while $C$ contains infinitely many elements:\n\n- A set containing a finite number of elements is called a **finite set**.\n- A set containing an infinite number of elements is called an **infinite set**.\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ are finite sets, while $C$ is an infinite set." :tutorial/id #uuid "eda65aa9-454a-53a2-a1cd-d218730af2b5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8213 :tutorial/title "Ellipsis Notation"} :step/id #uuid "93619eb6-b7dc-54fd-a1e0-a79da641bd64" :step/math-academy-id 86212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055911}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055911 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054428 :question/id #uuid "0684e366-bcf4-5b08-8eae-6600a88e2920" :question/math-academy-id "e-13137"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ceb6c84-a16b-5519-abd6-d2bb72ea81d3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233054}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Set Corresponding to a Description"} :step/id #uuid "cdaf05f4-d95a-5b13-bdac-bd7178402145" :step/math-academy-id 86213}] :activity/title "Sets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054904}]}
